\section{Check an Entailed Clause by Propagation}

Reverse unit propagation provides a compact checker for a
restricted kind of proof addition. The input is the current
finite CNF database and a proposed clause. Begin with a fresh
partial assignment falsifying every literal of the proposal. If
this assignment or subsequent unit propagation leads to a
contradiction, the database entails the clause and it can be
added. If propagation stops without a contradiction, do not add
the clause by this rule. That outcome does not establish that it
is false or that the database is satisfiable.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Clause database $D$, proposed clause $C$
\Let{A}{\operatorname{negatedLiterals}(C)}
\If{$A$ conflicts}
  \State \Return entails
\EndIf
\Repeat
  \To{Temporarily simplify without changing the database.}
  \Let{R}{\operatorname{simplify}(D,A)}
  \If{$R$ contains an empty clause}
    \State \Return entails
  \EndIf
  \To{A unit residual literal is forced under $A$.}
  \Let{u}{\operatorname{chooseUnit}(R)\text{ or none}}
  \State If $u$ exists, extend $A$ by $u$
\Until{$u$ is absent}
\State \Return not certified
\end{sudocode}

Use the three AND clauses and unit $r$, before adding
the query's unit $\neg b$. Propose adding $(b)$. Its negation
sets $b$ false; clause $(\neg r\lor b)$ then reduces to
$\neg r$. The unit $r$ conflicts with that forced literal,
and the proposal is accepted. Propose $(\neg a)$ instead.
The temporary assumption sets $a$ true and the database
propagates $r=b=\mathrm{true}$; there is no conflict. Do
not add that clause. This test also checks that assumptions
from the first attempted addition are not retained in the
next propagation.

Each extension preserves all possible satisfying completions
of the database plus the starting assumptions, so an empty
residual clause proves that no such completion exists.
There are at most as many extensions as variables in the
database; rescanning every literal at each update is simple
but often slower than watching literals in clauses. A complete
proof checker must still check the expected final result:
accepting several entailed clauses is not yet a refutation
unless the empty clause is derived.

\section{The Boundary of Certificate Checking}

Production proof logs need not list every binary resolution. For example, for a reverse-unit-propagation addition, temporarily assume the negation of each literal of the proposed clause. If unit propagation then reaches contradiction in the current database, the clause is entailed. Some formats permit more general redundancy steps that preserve satisfiability rather than logical equivalence of the databases. Each format requires its own precisely implemented checking rules, especially around deletions and preprocessing. Norbert Manthey's \emph{The MergeSat Solver} illustrates the engineering distinction: it can validate a reported model against the original formula and check a DRAT proof during solver execution.

Archive the exact input clauses, the variable-to-observation map, the certificate, and any assumptions used for an incremental query. A valid refutation for a stronger formula obtained by adding assumptions does not establish inconsistency of the base formula. A malformed proof or a checker timeout leaves the result unvalidated. The dossier's \emph{Compiler as a Constraint Solver} similarly distinguishes selecting an assignment from validating it. For a compiler, a proof-producing solver can remove search from the trusted base, but it cannot repair an encoding that omitted a behavior the language permits.
